parseInt returns a primitive int, so it can be added directly:

```java
public static int sum(String[] nums) {
    int total = 0;
    for (String s : nums)
        total += Integer.parseInt(s);
    return total;
}
```

versions.answer:
parseDouble returns a primitive double:

```java
public static double average(String[] nums) {
    double total = 0;
    for (String s : nums)
        total += Double.parseDouble(s);
    return total / nums.length;
}
```

valueOf returns Integer objects, and compareTo returns a positive number when the first is greater:

```java
public static Integer largest(String[] nums) {
    Integer max = Integer.valueOf(nums[0]);
    for (String s : nums) {
        Integer v = Integer.valueOf(s);
        if (v.compareTo(max) > 0) max = v;
    }
    return max;
}
```

original.answer:
```java
public boolean empty() { return size == 0; }
public int push(int value) { return elements[size++] = value; }
public int pop() { return elements[--size]; }
```

size is the number of elements and also the next free index, so push stores at size then increments; pop decrements first and returns that element.

versions.answer:
```java
public int peek() { return elements[size - 1]; }
public int getSize() { return size; }
```

peek returns the top element at index size - 1 without changing size; getSize returns size.

```java
public StackOfIntegers() { this(16); }
public StackOfIntegers(int capacity) { elements = new int[capacity]; }
```

The no-arg constructor calls the other with this(16).

